					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="2303803"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1481847491 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">16 Dec 2016 00:18</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2710257">
									

<p>I was liking it for about the first half, humor included. However, I feel like there were simply too many pop culture references shoehorned in by the end of it - to a point that made the narrative unwieldy to say the least. This reads to me like the author watched Lone Digger's music video and said &quot;what if this happened, but it was UIU-on-god violence instead of anthro creatures-on-anthro creatures violence.&quot; (I'm not saying that's literally what happened, I'm just saying that that's how it reads in terms of narrative significance.) By the end, I'm not sure what was accomplished other than handling a cat god I know nothing about and making a bunch of furry jokes.</p>
<p>In conclusion, maybe I'm missing something - even though I remember these characters from your earlier works, as you referenced. Maybe the cat god figure was already in this tale's settings in previous installments and I just forgot (I haven't read anything with these characters in a while, if I'm thinking of the right series as-is).</p>
<p>I'm usually pretty far from the type of site member that dislikes pieces simply for relying on the context of cultural niches, but this is definitely not doing it for me.</p>

							</div>

								
